simple if

if [ expression ]; then
 commands
fi


if else
# If the expression is true then it will execute commands

if [ expression ]; then
  commands
else
  other_commands
fi

else if # multiple expressions
# If the expression is true then it will execute commands, Else execute other_commands

if [ expression1 ]; then
  commands1
elif [ expression2 ]; then
  commands2
else
  commands3
fi

# If the we have multiple expressions then we will go for else-if


Expressions in Conditions
Expressions fall under three categories:
String Checks
Number Checks
File checks
String Checks
Operators:
= (equal)
!= (not equal)
-z (string empty)
-n (string not empty)
# [ string1 = string2 ] - true if both the strings are same
# [ string1 != string2 ] - true if both the strings are not same
# [ -z $var ] - true if $var is empty
# [ -n $var ] - true if $var is not empty

Number Checks
Operators:
-eq (equal)
-ne (not equal)
-lt (less than)
-le (less than or equal)
-gt (greater than)
-ge (greater than or equal)
# [ num1 operator number2 ]
# Operators:
# -eq , -ne , -gt, -ge , -lt , -le
# ex: [ 1 -lt 2 ]

File Checks
Common file checks:
-e FILE — file exists
-f FILE — regular file
-d DIRECTORY — directory exists

[ -e file ] - true if file exists
[ -f file ] - true if file exists and if it is a regular file
[ -d file ] - true if file exists and if it is a directory


